1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,992
Aarav opens a robot kit.

2
00:00:01,992 --> 00:00:04,134
The box has only one kind of chip.

3
00:00:04,434 --> 00:00:06,532
Every chip is a NAND gate.

4
00:00:06,532 --> 00:00:09,365
But his plan needs AND, OR and NOT.

5
00:00:09,665 --> 00:00:12,068
Can one kind of gate do every job?

6
00:00:12,068 --> 00:00:13,186
Let's find out.

7
00:00:13,686 --> 00:00:14,591
First, NAND.

8
00:00:14,591 --> 00:00:16,426
The name is short for NOT AND.

9
00:00:16,776 --> 00:00:19,768
Take an AND gate, and put
a NOT gate right after it.

10
00:00:20,118 --> 00:00:23,873
The symbol is the AND shape, with
a small circle at the output.

11
00:00:24,223 --> 00:00:26,611
That small circle is called a bubble.

12
00:00:26,611 --> 00:00:27,634
It means NOT.

13
00:00:28,284 --> 00:00:30,993
Without the bubble, it
would just be an AND gate.

14
00:00:31,643 --> 00:00:34,255
Let's fill its truth
table on the breadboard.

15
00:00:34,605 --> 00:00:35,673
Both inputs zero.

16
00:00:35,673 --> 00:00:36,658
AND gives zero.

17
00:00:36,658 --> 00:00:37,560
NOT flips it.

18
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:38,720
The output is one.

19
00:00:39,070 --> 00:00:40,095
Zero and one.

20
00:00:40,095 --> 00:00:40,995
Output one.

21
00:00:40,969 --> 00:00:41,995
One and zero.

22
00:00:41,995 --> 00:00:42,895
Output one.

23
00:00:43,218 --> 00:00:44,263
Both inputs one.

24
00:00:44,263 --> 00:00:45,222
AND gives one.

25
00:00:45,222 --> 00:00:46,139
NOT flips it.

26
00:00:46,139 --> 00:00:47,361
The output is zero.

27
00:00:47,711 --> 00:00:50,429
So NAND is zero only when
all its inputs are one.

28
00:00:50,779 --> 00:00:54,193
It is the exact opposite
of AND, row by row.

29
00:00:54,543 --> 00:00:58,748
We write Y equals A dot B, with
one bar over the whole thing.

30
00:00:59,398 --> 00:01:00,298
Next, NOR.

31
00:01:00,218 --> 00:01:01,704
It is short for NOT OR.

32
00:01:01,704 --> 00:01:03,276
An OR gate, then a NOT.

33
00:01:03,626 --> 00:01:04,742
Both inputs zero.

34
00:01:04,742 --> 00:01:05,727
OR gives zero.

35
00:01:05,727 --> 00:01:06,627
Flip it.

36
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:07,611
The output is one.

37
00:01:07,961 --> 00:01:10,519
If any input is one, OR gives one.

38
00:01:10,519 --> 00:01:12,916
Flip it, and the output is zero.

39
00:01:13,266 --> 00:01:16,139
So NOR is one only when
all its inputs are zero.

40
00:01:16,489 --> 00:01:20,731
We write Y equals A plus B, with
one bar over the whole thing.

41
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:25,015
The NOR symbol is the OR shape,
with the same small bubble.

42
00:01:25,665 --> 00:01:26,991
Now the big idea.

43
00:01:26,991 --> 00:01:29,425
Why are these called universal gates?

44
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:33,109
Because NAND gates alone
can build every other gate.

45
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:34,781
So can NOR gates alone.

46
00:01:35,431 --> 00:01:39,715
So a whole circuit can be made from
one simple gate, copied many times.

47
00:01:40,365 --> 00:01:41,265
First, NOT.

48
00:01:41,154 --> 00:01:43,304
Join both inputs of a NAND gate together.

49
00:01:43,654 --> 00:01:45,986
If A is zero, both inputs are zero.

50
00:01:45,986 --> 00:01:47,242
The output is one.

51
00:01:47,592 --> 00:01:49,867
If A is one, both inputs are one.

52
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:51,187
The output is zero.

53
00:01:51,187 --> 00:01:52,562
That is a NOT gate.

54
00:01:53,212 --> 00:01:54,234
Next, AND.

55
00:01:54,234 --> 00:01:57,066
Use a NAND gate, then a NAND as NOT.

56
00:01:57,066 --> 00:01:58,634
The two bars cancel.

57
00:01:59,284 --> 00:02:00,184
Then OR.

58
00:01:59,987 --> 00:02:01,822
Flip each input with a NAND.

59
00:02:01,822 --> 00:02:03,985
Then feed both into one more NAND.

60
00:02:04,335 --> 00:02:05,847
Out comes A plus B.

61
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:10,244
So NAND gives NOT with one gate,
AND with two, and OR with three.

62
00:02:10,894 --> 00:02:14,171
NOR works the same way,
with AND and OR swapped.

63
00:02:14,521 --> 00:02:16,212
OR needs two NOR gates.

64
00:02:16,212 --> 00:02:17,436
AND needs three.

65
00:02:18,086 --> 00:02:20,116
One kind of gate can build them all.

66
00:02:20,916 --> 00:02:21,995
Worked example.

67
00:02:21,995 --> 00:02:24,576
Build an AND gate using only NOR gates.

68
00:02:24,926 --> 00:02:27,854
First, flip A with a NOR
gate whose inputs are joined.

69
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:28,873
Out comes A bar.

70
00:02:29,573 --> 00:02:31,216
Flip B the same way.

71
00:02:31,216 --> 00:02:32,584
Out comes B bar.

72
00:02:33,284 --> 00:02:36,657
Now feed A bar and B bar
into a third NOR gate.

73
00:02:37,357 --> 00:02:39,198
Check with A one and B one.

74
00:02:39,198 --> 00:02:41,231
A bar and B bar are both zero.

75
00:02:41,931 --> 00:02:43,797
NOR of zero and zero is one.

76
00:02:43,797 --> 00:02:44,816
Just like AND.

77
00:02:44,816 --> 00:02:46,349
Three NOR gates in all.

78
00:02:46,999 --> 00:02:50,638
With NAND gates, the same
AND job takes only two gates.

79
00:02:51,288 --> 00:02:52,749
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:02:53,099 --> 00:02:55,826
Many students write
NAND as A bar dot B bar.

81
00:02:55,826 --> 00:02:57,668
A separate bar on each input.

82
00:02:58,018 --> 00:03:00,350
Test it with A one, and B zero.

83
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:02,330
A bar dot B bar gives zero.

84
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:04,166
But NAND gives one.

85
00:03:04,166 --> 00:03:07,521
The bar must cover the
whole product, A dot B.

86
00:03:08,171 --> 00:03:09,853
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:09,853 --> 00:03:14,119
In PUC board papers, and
in ISC Computer Science.

88
00:03:14,469 --> 00:03:18,896
JEE Main papers have also asked
for the output of a NAND gate.

89
00:03:19,246 --> 00:03:23,195
Expect truth tables, and building
other gates from NAND or NOR.

90
00:03:23,845 --> 00:03:24,745
Try this.

91
00:03:24,562 --> 00:03:26,379
A and B go into a NOR gate.

92
00:03:26,379 --> 00:03:29,131
Its output goes to both
inputs of a NAND gate.

93
00:03:29,131 --> 00:03:31,421
Which single gate is the whole circuit?

94
00:03:36,821 --> 00:03:37,721
C.

95
00:03:37,165 --> 00:03:38,065
An OR gate.

96
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:41,351
The NOR gate gives A plus B, with a bar.

97
00:03:41,701 --> 00:03:43,823
A NAND with joined inputs is a NOT gate.

98
00:03:43,823 --> 00:03:44,918
It removes the bar.

99
00:03:45,268 --> 00:03:47,204
What is left is A plus B.

100
00:03:47,204 --> 00:03:48,716
That is an OR gate.

101
00:03:49,366 --> 00:03:50,912
Screenshot this card.

102
00:03:54,112 --> 00:03:56,591
More PUC Electronics is coming soon.

103
00:03:56,591 --> 00:03:59,306
Follow the playlist, so you don't miss it.
